  "solution": {
    "text": "<math> <mrow> <mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mtext>n</mtext> </mfenced><mo>&nbsp;:&nbsp;</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mo>!</mo></math><br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mtext>1</mtext> </mfenced><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mn>2</mn> </mfenced><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mn>3</mn> </mfenced></mrow> </math> are not true.<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mtext>4</mtext> </mfenced><mo>&nbsp;:&nbsp;</mo></mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>24</mn></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;is true<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mtext>K</mtext> </mfenced></mrow> </math> : <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></msup></math> is true for some <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn></math><br /><br />To prove <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>:&nbsp;</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>!</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is true<br /><br />Multiplying both sides of <math> <mrow> <mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mtext>K</mtext> </mfenced></mrow> </math> by <math><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>),</mo></math> we get,<br /><br /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>!</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo></math><br /><br />Hence by <math> <mrow> <mtext>PMI,</mtext></mrow> </math> <math> <mrow> <mtext>P</mtext><mfenced> <mtext>n</mtext> </mfenced></mrow> </math> is true <math><mo>∀</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math>"
  }
